<%# pb-24 rather than pb-12, and the number is the footer's. #277 pinned the rating scale to
    the bottom of the screen, and a fixed element is out of the flow -- so without padding
    the same height beneath it, it would sit on top of the last set row of every session.
    The one control it would cover is Done, which is the whole point of the screen.

    24 is 96px against a collapsed footer of 44px plus its safe-area inset, which is more
    than the arithmetic needs. That slack is deliberate: it is the gap between the last Done
    button and the footer, and a thumb aiming at the first needs to not find the second. %>
<%# The id is the rest timer's handle on this element (#281). That bar is fixed, so when it
    is on screen it covers whatever the page ends with -- the script adds bottom padding
    here while it shows and takes it away again afterwards. Selected by id rather than by
    its class run, which would make these three utilities load-bearing and break the timer
    silently the next time the padding was tuned. %>
<div id="session-page" class="mx-auto max-w-md pb-24">
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between">
    <%# The day it was trained, falling back to the day it is written for. #572, and the same
        rule the record page and the calendar use -- see Workout#performed_or_planned_on.
        It matters more here than anywhere, because this is the screen somebody is holding
        while they train: a session opened in the gym and headed with Friday, two days from
        now, reads as having opened the wrong one. %>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <%= @workout.performed_or_planned_on.strftime('%A %b %-d') %>
    </h1>
    <%# "record" was the noun -- the page that records this session -- and read as the verb,
        which on a gym screen is an instruction to start recording. It is also where you go
        when you have stopped lifting, so it says that instead (#216).

        It only navigated, though, and #218 is what that cost: nothing in the app could say
        a session was over, so a day finished with three of ten sets done looked exactly
        like one still under way -- to the workouts list, and to an assistant reading the
        history through MCP, which called it in progress. The words were already right. Now
        the control does what they say, and lands on the record either way, which is where
        the link went before.

        A button rather than a link because it writes, and a form rather than htmx because
        this is the one control on the screen whose whole job is to leave it. Styled as the
        link it replaces: it sits beside an h1 as a quiet way out, not as the thing the
        page is for -- that is Done, five times over, below. %>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/finish" method="post">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/session/finish") %>
      <button type="submit" class="min-h-11 text-sm text-gray-500 underline hover:text-lime-500">
        <%= @workout[:finished_at] ? 'finished' : 'finish workout' %>
      </button>
    </form>
  </div>

  <div id="session-body">
    <%== render('workouts/_session_body') %>
  </div>

  <%# A way to put a lift on this session, which until #578 this screen had nowhere at all.

      The screenshot on that issue is a session reading "0 of 0 sets" offering "finish workout"
      and "Add a note" and nothing else. The link to /sets/new existed on the record page and
      on the set list -- both a page away -- so the gym floor screen was built on the
      assumption that a session already has sets in it, which is true of every session a
      programme wrote lifts onto and false of every other kind. A programme day with nothing
      written on it still gets a session generated for it, and `login_destination` lands a
      lifter on that session's screen the moment they sign in on the day. Empty, it was a
      screen you could only finish or annotate.

      **Always, rather than only when the session is empty**, and that is the judgement in
      #578 rather than a failure to notice how spare this screen is.

      A control that appeared on an empty session would be a control that vanished the instant
      it had been used -- the session is no longer empty, so the door out of it closes behind
      the first lift, at precisely the moment a lifter who has just written one movement in by
      hand wants to write the second. It would also be page furniture that comes and goes as
      the session changes under a poll, which is the thing the RPE footer below is placed the
      way it is to avoid. The empty session is not a separate case needing a separate control;
      it is the degenerate end of one need, which is that the session in the app should be able
      to become the session that is actually happening.

      The panels already answer the smaller half of this: each carries "Add a set" of the
      movement it is about (#496). What none of them can offer is a movement this session does
      not have yet, and that is a real decision made on the floor -- the rack is taken, a lift
      is added on a whim, the day was written empty. Distinct words for the distinct thing: a
      lift is what a panel is (session_lifts), so "Add a lift" is the one this makes, and #338
      is the reason the two must not both be called "Add a set" -- a list of this page's
      controls already reads "Done, button" a dozen times over.

      **Outside #session-body, which is where the care in #578 is owed.** Every Done tap sends
      back a lift panel and the poll sends back all of them, both into that region; a link
      rendered in there would be replaced on every tap -- or, where the response does not carry
      it, quietly removed from the page on the first one. Out here htmx never touches it. That
      is the same placement, and the same sentence, as the RPE footer, the rest timer, the
      nudge and the offline banner below.

      In the flow rather than pinned, unlike those four. The foot of this screen already stacks
      four fixed things that know each other's heights, and a fifth would be either a
      recalculation of all of them or a control sitting on top of a Done button. Nothing here
      is worth that: this is read once, at the start of a session or on a whim in the middle of
      one, and it is never the thing a thumb is aiming at between sets.

      Below the panels and above the note, which is the order the two already argue for. The
      note's own comment puts it below the panels because the panels are what the screen is
      for; this belongs above it for the same reason, being an action on the session's contents
      rather than an annotation about them.

      `return_to=session` is the other half of not being a dead end. The form redirects to the
      set it has just written, whose only way onwards is the record page, which is two taps
      from the session somebody is standing in the middle of -- so reached from here it comes
      back here instead. A fixed word rather than a path, so that nothing a request says can
      decide where a redirect goes. %>
  <a href="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/new?return_to=session"
     class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 flex min-h-11 w-full items-center justify-center border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3 text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">
    Add a lift
  </a>

  <%# Below the session rather than above it, and outside #session-body. #452.

      Outside because #session-body is replaced on every Done tap and every fifteen-second
      poll, and a textarea inside it would lose what was being typed each time -- which on
      this screen is every few seconds. This is the same argument the RPE footer below makes
      about its <details>, and it bites harder here because the state at risk is words
      somebody is in the middle of writing.

      Below because the lift panels are what the screen is for. A note is written once, at
      the end or after a bad set; the panels are read between every set. %>
  <%== render('workouts/_session_note') %>
</div>

<%# The rating scale, once, pinned to the foot of the screen. #277.

    Here rather than inside #session-body, and that is the placement the whole thing rests
    on: a Done tap sends back one lift panel and the progress bar, and the poll sends back
    every panel, so anything inside that region is replaced several times a session. A
    <details> is stateful -- it is open or it is not -- and state inside a swapped element
    is state that resets. Out here it is rendered once per page load and htmx never touches
    it, so a scale the lifter opened stays open through a set, a tap, and a poll.

    Asked of the whole session rather than of one lift, which is the guard that used to live
    at the foot of _lift_panel.erb. Same predicate -- ratable? on the model, so the table and
    the five buttons that fill it in can never disagree about who is being asked -- but read
    across every set on the page, because a footer belongs to the page.

    A session with nothing ratable in it therefore has no footer at all: all warmups, or all
    timed work, and the table would be explaining a scale nothing on screen offers. That
    session also keeps the shorter bottom padding it needs, which is the one thing the wrapper
    above cannot know -- and is why pb-24 is unconditional there rather than clever here. A
    little dead space at the foot of a warmup-only session is worth less than a rule that
    reads two ways. %>
<%- if @sets.any?(&:ratable?) %>
  <%== render('workouts/_rpe_help') %>
<%- end %>

<%# And whether any of the above is reaching the server at all. #516.

    Out here with the rest timer and the RPE footer, for the third time and for the same
    reason -- #session-body is replaced on every Done tap and every fifteen-second poll --
    though it bites differently here. What is at risk is not a countdown or an open
    disclosure but a report about the requests that swap this region: a banner living inside
    the thing it reports on would be replaced by every request that succeeded and left alone
    by every request that failed, which is the right answer for the wrong reason and stops
    being the right answer the moment anything else on the page swaps.

    Before the rest timer rather than after it because this is the one message that explains
    why the rest timer never started. %>
<%== render('workouts/_offline_banner') %>
<%== render('workouts/_session_stream') %>

<%# The rest timer and the element the server cues it through. #281.

    Out here with the RPE footer and for the identical reason, which is worth stating rather
    than leaving to the shared placement: #session-body is replaced on every Done tap and
    every fifteen-second poll, and a countdown is state. Inside that region the timer would
    restart four times a minute. Rendered once out here, htmx never touches it.

    The cue is rendered empty on first paint. A session opened an hour after its last set
    should not greet you with a rest timer, so the script marks whatever stamp it finds at
    load as already seen and waits for a tap to bring it a new one. %>
<%== render('workouts/_rest_cue', locals: { seconds: nil, kind: nil, movement: nil, at: nil, set_id: nil }) %>
<%== render('workouts/_rest_timer') %>

<%# And the question about whether the session is over at all. #410.

    Out here with the timer, for the identical reason: how long the screen has been quiet is
    state, and #session-body is replaced on every tap and every poll.

    Its cue is rendered *filled in* on first paint, which is where it differs from the rest
    cue above. A session opened an hour after its last set should not greet you with a rest
    timer -- but it absolutely should be asked whether it is finished, because that is exactly
    the session #410 is about. %>
<%== quiet_cue %>
<%== render('workouts/_session_nudge') %>

<%# One thing left in here, and it is not the one that used to be.
    A tap used to replace the whole of #session-body, which destroyed the scroller along
    with everything in it and took its scrollLeft with it -- so finishing a set on lift
    three dropped the lifter back on lift one, every set, all session. This script existed
    to save that number across the swap and put it back, and its note explained at length
    why hx-preserve was the wrong answer.

    #235 made the swap a single lift panel instead. The scroller is no longer replaced, so
    its scroll position is never lost, and the whole save-and-restore went with the problem
    rather than being carried alongside it.

    What remains is marking which lift you are on in the progress bar, which is #212 and is
    a different job: the bar is re-rendered out of band on every tap, so the marks come back
    unset and have to be recomputed. Bound to document.body because htmx events bubble, so
    it binds once and survives every swap; and to document in the capture phase for scroll,
    because scroll events do not bubble at all.

    Which panel is centred is a scroll position, and CSS cannot see one, so this is set from
    JS. It degrades to the bar exactly as it was: without the script every segment is
    unmarked, which is what it looked like before, and the per-panel dots and "3 of 5" are
    rendered server-side and go on saying where you are regardless. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    function markCurrentLift() {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      var sections = panels ? panels.querySelectorAll('section') : [];
      var segments = document.querySelectorAll('[data-lift-segment]');
      if (!panels || !sections.length || !segments.length) { return; }
      var width = sections[0].getBoundingClientRect().width;
      if (!width) { return; }
      var current = Math.round(panels.scrollLeft / width);
      for (var i = 0; i < segments.length; i++) {
        segments[i].setAttribute('data-current', i === current ? 'true' : 'false');
      }
    }

    document.addEventListener('scroll', function (event) {
      if (event.target && event.target.id === 'lift-panels') { markCurrentLift(); }
    }, true);

    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', markCurrentLift);

    markCurrentLift();
  })();
</script>

<%# The session clock, ticking. #281.
    The number in the header is true when the server sends it and stale a second later, so
    this counts on from it. It does not fetch anything and does not know what time it is on
    the server: the element carries how many seconds had already elapsed, and this adds the
    seconds since the page was painted. A phone whose clock is wrong is therefore still
    right, which asking the browser for a wall-clock start time would not be.

    Re-baselined on every htmx swap rather than left running, because the header comes back
    out of band on every tap and on every poll -- with a fresh figure from the server, which
    is the authoritative one. Reading the element again after each swap is what keeps the
    script agreeing with the page rather than drifting away from it over a long session.

    Bound to document.body because htmx events bubble, so it binds once and survives every
    swap. The same reasoning as the script above it, and the reason both live out here.

    Degrades to exactly what the server sent: with JavaScript off the header shows the
    session length as at page load, which is a true statement about a real session rather
    than a blank. That is why the figure is rendered server-side at all.

    The format is deliberately the same shape as Timing.phrase, and deliberately not shared
    with it -- one is Ruby on the server and one is JavaScript in the browser, and the only
    way to have one copy would be to ship a number and a formatter over the wire. The two
    can drift; spec/timing_spec.rb pins the Ruby half, and the server re-renders on every
    tap, so a drift shows up on screen rather than hiding. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var clock = null, base = 0, painted = 0;

    function spell(total) {
      if (total < 120) { return total + 's'; }
      var minutes = Math.floor(total / 60);
      if (minutes < 60) { return minutes + 'm'; }
      return Math.floor(minutes / 60) + 'h ' + (minutes % 60) + 'm';
    }

    function rebase() {
      clock = document.getElementById('session-clock');
      if (!clock) { return; }
      base = parseInt(clock.getAttribute('data-elapsed'), 10) || 0;
      painted = Date.now();
    }

    function tick() {
      if (!clock || !clock.isConnected) { return; }
      clock.textContent = spell(base + Math.floor((Date.now() - painted) / 1000));
    }

    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', rebase);
    rebase();
    setInterval(tick, 1000);
  })();
</script>
